\par \bullet \tab Briefly define or explain all technical terms.
\par \bullet \tab Explain all acronyms (e.g., User Interface Management System (UIMS)).
\par \bullet \tab Avoid local references (e.g., not everyone knows all city names). If the local context is important to the material, describe it fully.
\par \bullet \tab Avoid "insider" comments. Ensure that your whole audience understands any reference whose meaning you do not describe (e.g., do not assume that everyone has used a Macintosh or a particular application.)
\par \bullet \tab Avoid using "plays on words" and puns. Understanding phrases such as "red herring" requires a cultural knowledge of English.
\par \bullet \tab Use unambiguous forms for representing such culturally localized things as times, dates, personal names, currencies, and numbers.
\par \bullet \tab Be clear when you are using humor. Irony, in particular, can be hard to detect if you are a non-native English speaker.
\par \bullet \tab Avoid the use of gender-specific pronouns ("he," "she") and other words ("chairman," "manpower"). Use inclusive language (e.g., "she or he," "they," "chair," "staff") that is gender-neutral.
